                      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-white/5 text-xs border border-white/10 hover:bg-white/10 cursor-pointer transition-colors">
                        <span>👀</span>
                        <span class="text-white/60">2</span>
                      </span>
                      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-white/5 text-xs border border-white/10 hover:bg-white/10 cursor-pointer transition-colors">
                        <span>🚨</span>
                        <span class="text-white/60">1</span>
                      </span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Thread Replies Container -->
                <div id="databases-thread-container" class="ml-12 space-y-3 hidden">
                </div>
              </div>

              <!-- Message Input Area -->
              <div class="px-4 py-3 border-t border-white/5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-2 rounded-lg bg-[#0f1114] border border-white/10">
                  <svg class="h-5 w-5 text-white/30" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 6v6m0 0v6m0-6h6m-6 0H6" />
                  </svg>
                  <span class="text-white/30 text-sm">Message #database-alerts</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Pricing & Self-Hosting Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-gray-50 py-24 sm:py-32">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center mb-16">
          <p class="text-sm font-medium text-emerald-600 uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">Pricing & Self-Hosting</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900 sm:text-4xl">
            Pay for data, or run it yourself
          </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">OneUptime Cloud</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Usage-based: $0.10 per GB of telemetry ingested, with retention you can set per database. There is no fee per database, instance or replica, so adding a read replica costs what its metrics weigh.</p>
            <ul class="mt-6 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
              <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="h-4 w-4 text-emerald-500 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" /></svg>No per-database or per-host licence</li>
              <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="h-4 w-4 text-emerald-500 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" /></svg>Query samples and top queries only when you switch them on</li>
              <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="h-4 w-4 text-emerald-500 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" /></svg>On-call, status pages, and incidents included</li>
            </ul>
            <a href="/pricing" class="mt-6 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm font-medium text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700">See pricing <svg class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M13.5 4.5L21 12m0 0l-7.5 7.5" /></svg></a>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Self-Hosted</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Databases is part of the open source Community Edition (Apache 2.0). Run it on your own Kubernetes cluster or Docker host, keep every query and metric inside your network, and point the Database Agent at your own instance instead of ours. Everything else is identical.</p>
            <ul class="mt-6 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
              <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="h-4 w-4 text-emerald-500 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" /></svg>Air-gapped and data-residency deployments supported</li>
              <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="h-4 w-4 text-emerald-500 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" /></svg>Discovery thresholds and the auto-create budget are tunable</li>
              <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="h-4 w-4 text-emerald-500 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" /></svg>Same agent, same pages, same recommended monitors</li>
            </ul>
            <a href="/enterprise/self-hosted" class="mt-6 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm font-medium text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700">Self-hosting guide <svg class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M13.5 4.5L21 12m0 0l-7.5 7.5" /></svg></a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- FAQ Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-white py-24 sm:py-32" id="databases-faq">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center mb-16">
          <p class="text-sm font-medium text-emerald-600 uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">FAQ</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900 sm:text-4xl">
            Questions database teams ask
          </h2>
        </div>

        <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Do I have to install anything on the database server?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">No. Discovery reads the traces, pods and containers OneUptime already receives. For engine metrics, the Database Agent is a stock <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">otel/opentelemetry-collector-contrib</code> container with a config for your engine. It runs on any Docker host, or as a small Deployment in Kubernetes, that can reach the database's port. One agent watches one database server.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">What permissions does the Database Agent need?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">A login of its own that can read the engine's statistics and nothing else, never an administrator or an application's login. That is <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">pg_monitor</code> on PostgreSQL, <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">VIEW SERVER STATE</code> on SQL Server, <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">SELECT_CATALOG_ROLE</code> on Oracle, <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">clusterMonitor</code> on MongoDB, and an ACL user limited to <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">INFO</code> and <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">PING</code> on Redis. None of them grant access to your data. The credentials stay beside the agent, in an owner-only <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">.env</code> file or a Kubernetes Secret.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Does it work with managed databases?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. A managed service that speaks an engine's protocol is that engine: its queries show up from your traces, and the Database Agent connects to it like any other server. Services reached through their provider's own API, such as DynamoDB, Cosmos DB, BigQuery and Spanner, are added by hand, and an OpenTelemetry Collector reads their metrics from the provider.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Will every test container and IP address become a database?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">No. Traces create a database only for an address that names one server across the project, and only after 10 queries in 15 minutes. Pods and containers have to stay up for about 10 minutes first. Connection poolers, backup tools, Testcontainers runs and one-off jobs are never databases, and in Kubernetes neither are client or debug runs of a database image. Automatic creation stops at 500 live discovered databases per project, and an untouched discovered database nobody has seen for 7 days is archived on its own.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Does the Database Agent send query text?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Only if you turn it on. Engine metrics are numbers. Query samples and top queries carry query text, and they stay off until you set <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">DATABASE_QUERY_EVENTS=true</code>. The Database Health monitor sends only named numbers, and when the Database AI agent returns query text to an investigation, every literal in it is replaced with <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">?</code>. Query text in your applications' own spans is up to your instrumentation.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">How does this relate to the Database Health and SQL Query monitors?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">They work together. Databases is the inventory: one page per server with everything known about it. A <a href="/docs/monitor/database-health-monitor" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700 font-medium">Database Health monitor</a> checks a server's health from a probe with no agent at all, and a <a href="/docs/monitor/sql-monitor" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700 font-medium">SQL Query monitor</a> alerts on your own query's result. When the host and port a monitor connects to is one of a database's endpoints, its alerts and incidents show on that database's page.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Our database runs on Kubernetes. How do its engine metrics join the page?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Run the Database Agent as a small Deployment in the database's namespace. The database's Documentation tab renders the manifest for you, prefilled with its namespace, engine, address and id. The id links the metrics straight to the detected database, even when the project has several clusters. Discovery on the cluster itself comes from the <a href="/product/kubernetes" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700 font-medium">Kubernetes agent</a> you already run.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Where do I start?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Create a project and send traces, or connect a Kubernetes cluster or a Docker or Podman host. Then open <strong class="font-medium text-gray-800">Resources → Databases</strong>. Each database's Documentation tab has its own install command and collector config. The <a href="/docs/telemetry/databases" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700 font-medium">Databases docs</a> cover discovery, the Database Agent, endpoints, alerting and troubleshooting.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

